LEX(1)                         P32W Unix                          LEX(1)

NAME
       lex - make a lexical analyser from regular expressions

SYNOPSIS
       lex [-t] [-n] [-v] [file ...]
       cc lex.yy.c -ll

DESCRIPTION
       lex reads a specification (the files in turn, or the standard
       input) and writes a scanner in C to lex.yy.c: a function
       yylex that reads its input, finds in it the patterns of the
       specification and runs the C code (the action) given for
       each. Compile it with cc; -ll adds a main that calls yylex
       until it returns 0, and a yywrap that returns 1 (each is
       taken only if the program has none of its own).

       A specification has three parts, separated by lines %%:

              definitions
              %%
              rules
              %%
              user code

       The definitions and the user code may be empty, and the
       second %% may be left out with the user code.

THE DEFINITIONS
       %{ ... %}
              lines copied into lex.yy.c before yylex (each mark
              at the start of a line of its own); also any line
              that starts with a blank, and /* comments */ that
              start a line
       NAME regex
              a definition: {NAME} in a pattern stands for regex,
              as if in ( )
       %s NAME ..., %start NAME ...
              inclusive start conditions (below)
       %x NAME ...
              exclusive start conditions
       %array, %pointer
              yytext is an array (char yytext[YYLMAX], 8192 unless
              YYLMAX is defined) or a pointer into the input
              (the default)
       %option word ...
              noyywrap (yylex does not call yywrap: the end of
              the input is the end), main (a main of its own;
              implies noyywrap), nodefault (text no rule matches
              is an error instead of being copied), case-insensitive
              (or caseless: letters match either case), array,
              pointer, yylineno (always on here); others are
              ignored, with a warning for the ones lex does not
              know
       %e %p %n %k %a %o
              table sizes, for old lex: ignored

THE RULES
       Each rule is a pattern at the start of a line, then blanks,
       then its action: a C statement on the rest of the line, or
       a block { ... } that may go on over several lines, or |
       (the action of the next rule). With no action, the text is
       thrown away. Lines starting with a blank before the first
       rule, and %{ ... %} there, are put at the start of yylex
       (declarations of variables, say).

       A pattern may start with <SC>, <SC1,SC2> or <*>: it is
       active only in those start conditions (<*>: all of them).
       Otherwise it is active in INITIAL and every inclusive (%s)
       start condition. <SC>{ on a line of its own starts a scope:
       the rules up to the matching } (alone on its line) have <SC>
       before them, and may be indented.

PATTERNS
       Extended regular expressions, with lex's additions:

              c       a character other than \ " . [ ] ( ) { } * + ?
                      | / ^ $ < >
              \c      \n \t \r \f \v \a \b, \ooo (octal), \xhh
                      (hex); any other c is itself
              "..."   the characters as they are (\ escapes work)
              [...]   any character of the class: ranges a-z,
                      [:alpha:] [:digit:] [:alnum:] [:upper:]
                      [:lower:] [:space:] [:blank:] [:punct:]
                      [:print:] [:graph:] [:cntrl:] [:xdigit:]
              [^...]  any character not in it (newline too)
              .       any character but newline
              {NAME}  the definition NAME
              r* r+ r?    zero or more, one or more, zero or one
              r{n} r{n,} r{n,m}   n, at least n, n to m times
                      (counts up to 255)
              rs r|s (r)  r then s, r or s, grouping
              ^r      r at the start of a line (only at the start
                      of a pattern)
              r$      r at the end of a line (only at the end of
                      a pattern): the same as r/\n
              r/s     r, but only when s follows it (trailing
                      context: s is matched, then given back)

       A pattern ends at the first blank outside "..." and [...].

MATCHING
       yylex matches the longest text it can; if two rules match
       the same longest text, the earlier rule wins. The length
       counts the trailing context of r/s. Text no rule matches is
       copied to yyout a character at a time.

IN THE ACTIONS
       yytext, yyleng
              the text matched and its length
       yylineno
              the line number (counted always: 1 plus the newlines
              matched so far)
       yyin, yyout
              the FILE * yylex reads and ECHO writes (the standard
              input and output unless set before)
       ECHO   write yytext to yyout
       BEGIN SC, BEGIN(SC), BEGIN(INITIAL)
              go into start condition SC; YY_START is the current
              one
       REJECT go on to the next rule that matched this text (in
              rule order), then to shorter matches
       yymore()
              keep yytext: the next match is added to its end
       yyless(n)
              keep the first n characters; the rest is matched
              again
       input()
              read the next character (EOF at the end)
       unput(c)
              put c back, to be read next (yytext is kept)
       yyterminate()
              return 0 from yylex
       return value
              a rule's action may return; yylex returns that
              value, and the next call goes on from there

       yywrap() is called at the end of the input. If it returns
       0 (having set yyin to another file, say), yylex goes on
       reading; if 1, yylex returns 0. yyrestart(file) starts
       reading file afresh. YY_INPUT(buf, result, max) may be
       defined in the definitions to read input some other way
       (result: the characters put in buf, 0 at the end); YY_DECL,
       YY_USER_ACTION, YY_BREAK and ECHO may be defined too.

OPTIONS
       -t     write the scanner to the standard output instead of
              lex.yy.c
       -v     report the numbers of rules, NFA and DFA states,
              character classes and table entries on the standard
              error
       -n     do not (the default)

EXAMPLES
       Count the lines, words and characters of the input, as wc:

              %{
              int lines, words, chars;
              %}
              %%
              \n          { lines++; chars++; }
              [^ \t\n]+   { words++; chars += yyleng; }
              .           { chars++; }
              %%
              int yywrap(void)
              {
                  printf("%d %d %d\n", lines, words, chars);
                  return 1;
              }

       $ lex count.l
       $ cc -o count lex.yy.c -ll
       $ ./count < /etc/passwd

       Comments with an exclusive start condition, numbers and
       names:

              %x C
              %%
              "/*"            BEGIN(C);
              <C>"*/"         BEGIN(INITIAL);
              <C>.|\n         ;
              [0-9]+          printf("number %s\n", yytext);
              [a-z_][a-z0-9_]* printf("name %s\n", yytext);
              .|\n            ;

FILES
       lex.yy.c            the scanner
       /usr/lib/libl.a     main and yywrap (cc -ll)

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for an error in the specification (lex names the file
       and line) or a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       cc(1), sed(1), grep(1)

BUGS
       There are no <<EOF>> rules (use yywrap), no yy_push_state,
       no multiple buffers (yy_create_buffer and the like), no %top
       and no --long options; -n does nothing.

       Trailing context r/s is exact when s, or else r, matches
       text of one length only. When both vary, yytext ends at the
       last place r could have ended that leaves room for the
       shortest s, which is wrong when r and s overlap (as in
       a*/a*b; flex has the same fault); at most 31 rules may have
       such trailing context. When r matches no text at all (x*/y
       on y), the rule is not used and the character is copied
       (flex loops for ever); lex warns of such patterns.

       input(), unput() and yyless() exist only if the
       specification mentions them (lex looks for the words).
       yylineno is always counted, even without %option yylineno.

       The native cc needs some 40 seconds of P32W time for a small
       scanner and a minute or more for a large one, much of it on
       the tables: keywords looked up in a table, as names, cost
       fewer states than a rule for each.
